_type":"markdown","source":"**4.\tHow do the values of the slope of the peak exercise ST segment (slope) vary with different chest pain types?**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"slope_by_pain = (\n    data_clean.groupby(\"cp\", observed=True)[\"slope\"]\n    .value_counts()\n    .unstack(fill_value=0)\n)\n\nslope_by_pain.index = [\"Type \" + str(i) for i in slope_by_pain.index]\nslope_by_pain.columns = [\"Slope \" + str(i) for i in slope_by_pain.columns]\n\ndisplay(slope_by_pain)\n\nslope_by_pain.plot(kind=\"bar\", stacked=True, figsize=(9, 6))\n\nplt.title(\"Slope Distribution by Chest Pain Type\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Chest Pain Type\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Number of Patients\")\nplt.xticks(rotation=0)\nplt.legend(title=\"Slope\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-15T09:39:36.735067Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-15T09:39:36.735395Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-15T09:39:36.916683Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-15T09:39:36.735371Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-15T09:39:36.915565Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"        Slope 0  Slope 1  Slope 2\nType 0       11       84       48\nType 1        2       12       36\nType 2        5       33       48\nType 3        3       11        9","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Slope 0</th>\n      <th>Slope 1</th>\n      <th>Slope 2</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Type 0</th>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>84</td>\n      <td>48</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Type 1</th>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>36</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Type 2</th>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>33</td>\n      <td>48</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Type 3</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>9</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x600 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":105},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"*The slope of the peak exercise ST segment varies across chest pain types. For Type 0 and Type 3, slope 1 is the most common, with 84 and 11 patients. For Type 1 and Type 2, slope 2 is the most common, with 36 and 48 patients. Slope 0 is rare in every group. This shows that chest pain types are linked to different ECG patterns, which can help improve diagnosis.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**5.\tAnalyze the survival rates of patients with different thalassemia types over a period.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# thal is changed to a number only for this plot so the average can be calculated\nplot_data = data_clean.copy()\nplot_data[\"thal\"] = plot_data[\"thal\"].astype(int)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n\nsns.lineplot(\n    x=\"age\",\n    y=\"thal\",\n    hue=\"target\",\n    data=plot_data,\n    estimator=\"mean\",\n    errorbar=None\n)\n\nplt.title(\"Average Thalassemia Type by Age and Heart Disease Status\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Age (Years)\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Average Thalassemia Type\")\n\nplt.legend(\n    title=\"Heart Disease\",\n    labels=[\"No Heart Disease\", \"Heart Disease\"]\n)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# Average thal value for each group\nprint(\"Average thalassemia type by heart disease status:\")\ndisplay(plot_data.groupby(\"target\", observed=True)[\"thal\"].mean().round(2))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-15T09:39:36.917655Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-15T09:39:36.917992Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-15T09:39:37.142378Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-15T09:39:36.917969Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-15T09:39:37.140599Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x600 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Average thalassemia type by heart disease status:\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"target\n0    2.56\n1    2.13\nName: thal, dtype: float64"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":106},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"*The dataset has no follow up or survival data, so true survival rates cannot be measured. Using age in place of time, patients with heart disease mostly have a fixed defect (average thal 2.13), while those without heart disease have more reversible defects (average 2.56). This difference stays similar across ages, so there is no clear age trend.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Conclusion","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"*The analysis of 302 unique patients shows that the number of major vessels, chest pain type, exercise induced angina, oldpeak, maximum heart rate, thalassemia type and sex are most strongly linked to heart disease, while cholesterol, blood pressure and fasting blood sugar show weak relationships. The logistic regression model reached 85.2% accuracy and a ROC AUC of 0.906. Some results are the opposite of what is expected medically and may be due to how the target is coded, so they should be checked with clinical experts. The findings show association, not causation, but can help in identifying high risk patients and planning preventive care.*","metadata":{}}]}